{"name":"Inviting Gesture","hex":"#cdc29d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"inviting","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alluring; tempting; attractive."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of invite"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/inviting"},{"term":"gesture","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A motion of the limbs or body, especially one made to emphasize speech.","An act or a remark that serves as a formality or as a sign of attitude."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a gesture or gestures.","To express (something) by a gesture or gestures."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gesture"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gesture","blocks":[{"text":"A gesture is a form of nonverbal communication or non-vocal communication in which visible bodily actions communicate particular messages, either in place of, or in conjunction with, speech. Gestures include movement of the hands, face, or other parts of the body. Gestures differ from physical non-verbal communication that does not communicate specific messages, such as purely expressive displays, proxemics, or displays of joint attention. Gestures allow individuals to communicate a variety of feelings and thoughts, from contempt and hostility to approval and affection, often together with body language in addition to words when they speak. Gesticulation and speech work independently of each other, but join to provide emphasis and meaning."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Madonna_%40_Berlinale_%281%29_cropped.jpg/330px-Madonna_%40_Berlinale_%281%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gesture","wikidata":"Q371174"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Us_navy_helicopter_landing_signals_illustration.jpg/500px-Us_navy_helicopter_landing_signals_illustration.jpg